1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,127
Have you ever been asked to do something that felt a little scary - and said yes anyway?
Maybe it was speaking in front of people. Maybe it was trying something you'd never done
before. Maybe it was just walking into a room full of strangers.

2
00:00:13,127 --> 00:00:28,114
That feeling - that mix of nervous and excited - has a name. Over the next few minutes,
you're going to learn what it's called, why it matters, and why the program you're
starting today is built entirely around it. Welcome. You're about to begin something
called ACT.

3
00:00:28,114 --> 00:00:41,790
Let's start the way this program is meant to start: بِسْمِ اللَّهِ - in the name of Allah.
الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ - all praise is due to Allah. And صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ -
peace and blessings upon the Prophet. Thank you for being here.

4
00:00:41,790 --> 00:01:01,047
I want you to know something right away: this program is not going to feel like a
classroom. It's casual. It's informal. You're allowed to be yourself in it. What you're
starting is a series of exercises, activities, and games - and I want you to bring real
enthusiasm and initiative to all of it, because what you put in is what you'll get out.

5
00:01:01,047 --> 00:01:14,420
This program is called ACT - which stands for Attitude Cultivates Transformation. That's
exactly what it is: a program built on the idea that your attitude - how you choose to
face things - is what actually transforms you.

6
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:36,870
ACT is an interactive program designed to equip you with real skills for your future - the
kind of skills the job market actually asks for, and the kind that help you build your
career - through social and voluntary work. It wasn't built generically. It was designed
specifically with you in mind, and you've been chosen to be part of it, alongside other
young people who share the same bright future ahead of them.

7
00:01:36,870 --> 00:02:03,229
Before we go further, it's worth knowing who's behind this. ACT is a global initiative by
the NAMA Foundation. Working alongside them is Ghadan - a not-for-profit consultancy and
capacity-building foundation. Take a second and think about that phrase, 'not-for-profit.'
It means an organization that doesn't take any profit for its partners or stakeholders -
everything it does is aimed at its mission, which here is increasing social participation
in your community.

8
00:02:03,229 --> 00:02:16,776
There's also something ahead of you to look forward to: an awards ceremony, held across
Tanzania, where the strongest groups will be celebrated - with official guests in
attendance. So everything you do in this program can lead somewhere real.

9
00:02:16,776 --> 00:02:24,667
Here's the shape of the whole journey you're starting. ACT is built from four units, and
each one answers a different question and builds different skills.

10
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:38,580
Unit 1 is Self-Awareness. This is where you are right now. In this unit, you'll get to
know yourself - your identity, your strengths, your capabilities - through games and
activities that are actually engaging, not just informative.

11
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:52,969
Unit 2 is Social Awareness. Here, you'll become aware of your society - its strengths and
its limits - and understand what it really means to be part of a collective community.
You'll watch examples from other societies too, and keep learning through games.

12
00:02:52,969 --> 00:03:11,746
Unit 3 is Awareness of Social Issues. In this unit, you'll become aware of the social
issues that actually exist around you, understand how serious they are, and start thinking
about how to contribute to solving them. You'll build a sense of social justice, and by
the end, you'll choose a social issue you personally want to work on.

13
00:03:11,746 --> 00:03:28,712
Unit 4 is Social Initiatives. This is the biggest unit - it covers half of the whole
program, and it's often the most fun and rewarding part. Here you'll learn the real
fundamentals of planning and managing a project. You'll choose your own project, build it,
and share what you achieved with the people around you.

14
00:03:28,712 --> 00:03:39,955
Notice the shape of that journey: who am I, who are others, what do others struggle with,
and what can I actually offer? That's the path ACT is going to walk you through, one unit
at a time.

15
00:03:39,955 --> 00:03:54,066
Before we go any further, let me tell you something about the person guiding you through
this program - and I want you to test yourself a little. I'm going to tell you three
things about myself. Two are true. One is not. See if you can guess which one.

16
00:03:54,066 --> 00:04:25,641
Here's something trainers in this program love to do on day one: they share two true
things about themselves, and one lie - and let the room guess which is which. It's a
simple trick, but it works, because it turns being taught at into getting to know someone.
So here's a challenge for you: think of two true things about yourself, and one believable
lie. Keep them in your head, or better, write them down. You'll get a chance to try this
out on someone in your life today. That's the spirit this whole program runs on. So - how
excited are you to get started?

17
00:04:25,641 --> 00:04:47,251
Let's begin the way this program always begins: by breaking the ice. Grab a piece of paper
- or your workbook if you have one - and tear or cut it into three small pieces. On each
piece, write one thing about yourself: a hobby, a skill, or a personal trait. Just one per
piece. Take your time with this - it doesn't have to be impressive, it just has to be
true.

18
00:04:47,251 --> 00:05:14,216
Now it's your turn. Pause the video here. Write your three things - one hobby, one skill,
one trait. When you're done, come back and press play. If you're watching together with a
group: once everyone has their three papers, fold them, mix them together, and have each
person draw three at random, then go find who wrote each one. If you're watching alone,
keep your three papers somewhere you'll see them again - you'll be building on this idea
of 'who am I' throughout the program.

19
00:05:14,216 --> 00:05:29,295
That's a small moment, but it matters more than it seems. You just put into words three
real, specific things about who you are. Hold onto that. Self-awareness - which is exactly
what Unit 1 is about - starts with small, honest moments like this one.

20
00:05:29,295 --> 00:05:46,773
Now that we've broken the ice, let's talk about something important: the kind of space we
want this to be. Think about it for a second - what would you want from the people around
you, and from me, to make this program feel smooth and worthwhile? What would help you
feel comfortable enough to actually speak your mind here?

21
00:05:46,773 --> 00:06:11,568
Here are a few examples of the kinds of things that usually come up: putting phones away
except for real emergencies, showing up on time, and - maybe most importantly - nobody
making fun of anyone else's ideas or contributions. Think about it this way: what do you
think about people interrupting each other while they're talking? Should we agree not to
do that? And what about people who laugh at someone else's opinion while they're sharing
it?

22
00:06:11,568 --> 00:06:27,877
If you're watching this in a group: talk through these questions together, agree on your
own list of ground rules, write them down somewhere everyone can see, and have everyone
sign it. If you're watching alone: pause here and write down, in your own words, the two
or three things you personally commit to.

23
00:06:27,877 --> 00:06:38,832
Here's the important part: this isn't a list of rules being handed down to you. You are
the ones deciding how this program runs. That's what makes it a safe space - you have real
control over it.

24
00:06:38,832 --> 00:06:51,257
One more thing before we move on: a short survey. There are no grades on this - it's
simply a way to capture where your knowledge stands right now, so that by the end of this
journey, you can look back and see how far you've come.

25
00:06:51,257 --> 00:07:16,260
Now it's your turn. Pause the video, and rate yourself honestly, right now: I understand
what ACT is and what it's for. I could name at least one of the four units. I know what
the 'challenge zone' means. I feel confident stepping outside my comfort zone. I'm excited
to start this program. Answer honestly, keep your answers somewhere safe, and press play
when you're done. We'll come back to this at the very end of the program.

26
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:34,119
Alright. Now we get to the heart of this lesson - and honestly, to an idea that's going to
follow you through this entire program. Picture three circles. The first is your Comfort
Zone - call it 'Easy.' The second is your Challenge Zone - call it 'Let's Go.' And the
third is your Pain Zone - call it 'No Way!'

27
00:07:34,119 --> 00:07:58,310
Here's what each one actually means. The Easy zone is where you do things without needing
to think hard or put in any real effort - it's automatic. The Impossible zone is where you
run into something you genuinely don't know how to tackle, and it leaves you stuck. And
the Let's Go zone - the challenge zone - is where you have some idea how to tackle
something, but not yet enough experience to do it easily. That's exactly what makes it a
challenge.

28
00:07:58,310 --> 00:08:09,962
I'm going to walk you through five situations. For each one, I want you to actually
picture yourself in it, and decide - honestly - which zone you'd be in: Easy, Let's Go, or
No Way.

29
00:08:09,962 --> 00:08:21,760
First: Suppose you're on a trip with your teacher and your classmates, and suddenly the
plane develops a technical problem mid-flight. The pilot tells everyone to jump out with a
parachute. Which zone are you in?

30
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:31,074
Second: You have to dive into the ocean, and you're told there are sharks nearby - but the
locals say these particular sharks are peaceful and won't hurt you. Which zone?

31
00:08:31,074 --> 00:08:35,802
Third: You have to stand up and speak in front of 700 people. Which zone?

32
00:08:35,802 --> 00:08:41,260
Fourth: You're handed a strange meal, and you have no idea what's actually in it. Which
zone?

33
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:49,075
Fifth: You have to voice your honest opinion in front of important decision-makers - and
you already know they disagree with you. Which zone?

34
00:08:49,075 --> 00:09:05,990
Pause the video now, and for each of those five, decide which zone you landed in - and if
you can, think about why. If you're watching in a group: after each scenario, have
everyone move to a different part of the room representing their zone, and ask a few
people to explain their choice out loud.

35
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:25,604
Let's think through what just happened. What did you actually discover about yourself in
that exercise? Are the three zones clear to you now - comfort, challenge, and pain? What's
the real difference between them? Now think about how each zone feels. What do you feel
toward the comfort zone? Toward the challenge zone? Toward the pain zone?

36
00:09:25,604 --> 00:09:51,409
Here's a harder question. Why did some of those situations feel 'easy' to you, and not to
someone else? Why did some feel like 'Let's Go,' and others like 'impossible'? And think
back on your own life - have the times you felt genuinely challenged ever actually made
you stronger? Does staying in the comfort zone ever really grow your knowledge or your
power? Does the pain zone add anything to you at all? And does the challenge zone actually
benefit you, strengthen you?

37
00:09:51,409 --> 00:10:15,990
So here's the real question this whole exercise is pointing toward: which zone do we
actually need to spend our time in, if we want to keep growing and learning? A lot of
people get this wrong at first - it's easy to assume the answer is the comfort zone,
because it's comfortable. But the zone that actually grows your strengths and capabilities
- the zone that becomes the ladder toward becoming a capable, influential person - is the
challenge zone.

38
00:10:15,990 --> 00:10:27,198
God created us with the ability to develop and to advance. And the real difference between
an exceptional human being and an average one isn't talent - it's how much they've
actually used and benefited from that ability.

39
00:10:27,198 --> 00:10:35,569
The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'Paradise is surrounded by hardships.' And hardship
is about as close as it gets to the challenge zone.

40
00:10:35,569 --> 00:11:00,810
Think about it - a person can grow so much. Change for the better - psychologically,
spiritually, emotionally, even financially - just by taking one step: out of the comfort
zone, and into the challenge zone. Youth - the stage of life you're in right now - is the
very best period to learn, to face challenges, and to grow in strength, knowledge, and
power, for your own future and for the future of your community.

41
00:11:00,810 --> 00:11:21,428
This exercise, small as it seems, can genuinely change your life. And here's something
worth sitting with: many of us are serving our community, in some real way, for the first
time in our lives through this program. Children think enjoyment comes from taking. But
people who've actually lived know that the truest pleasures in life are the moments of
giving - not taking.

42
00:11:21,428 --> 00:11:31,306
This program - ACT - is built to deliberately put you in the challenge zone, again and
again, so that you walk out of it having genuinely added something new to who you are.

43
00:11:31,306 --> 00:11:53,254
Now let's see this in real life, not just as a theory. It's the story of the Koh Panyee
Island Soccer Team, from Thailand - a group of boys who built their own soccer field out
of nothing, in a place nobody expected a soccer team to come from. Pause the video now and
watch the short film linked below - 'The Koh Panyee Island Soccer Team.' When it's
finished, come back and press play.

44
00:11:53,254 --> 00:12:07,859
Take a moment with what you just watched. What did you actually see happen in that video?
How did people react when that soccer field was first built? Why do you think their
reactions changed once the team actually started playing - and how did it feel watching
them win?

45
00:12:07,859 --> 00:12:16,464
Why do you think those boys were able to beat so many different, more established teams?
And why were they able to keep playing hard, even through bad weather?

46
00:12:16,464 --> 00:12:37,834
Here's the truth underneath that story: if it hadn't been for the harsh, uneven ground
they had to play on - and the pain they had to push through - they would never have
developed the skill it took to beat those other teams under normal conditions. Hardship
and challenge are what build strong people. Whenever you're in the middle of a genuinely
tough situation, that's not a sign something's gone wrong. It's a sign you're growing.

47
00:12:37,834 --> 00:12:58,649
That same team went on to win seven championships in a row. Facing a challenge can be hard
at the beginning, and yes, you might even be mocked for it, the way those boys were mocked
for trying to play soccer on a wooden field on an island. But the same people who mocked
them became their biggest supporters, the moment they saw how skilled they'd become, and
how much they believed in themselves.

48
00:12:58,649 --> 00:13:19,915
If you want to be a leader in your community, hold onto that competitive mindset. Whenever
a challenge comes your way, ask yourself: what can I learn from this? What skill is going
to come out of the other side of this? Even everyday challenges - like having to work to
earn money - are quietly teaching you real, transferable skills that the job market values
everywhere in the world.

49
00:13:19,915 --> 00:13:49,706
Today, you were welcomed into ACT - a program built on the idea that your attitude is what
transforms you. You learned what this program actually is, who's behind it, and the four-
unit journey you're about to walk: who you are, who others are, what others struggle with,
and what you can offer. You helped build the ground rules for a space where you can
genuinely speak your mind. And most importantly, you were introduced to the three learning
zones, and the idea that real growth only happens when you deliberately step into the
challenge zone.

50
00:13:49,706 --> 00:14:03,669
Right now, if you can, say this to yourself - out loud if you're comfortable, or quietly
if you're not: 'I am a change maker. I live in the challenge zone.' If you're watching in
a group: gather in a circle and say this together, in unison.

51
00:14:03,669 --> 00:14:13,545
Hold onto that. Because in the next lesson, we're going to go even deeper into the first
real question of this journey - the question of who you actually are. I'll see you there.

